  "query_preview": "..Today...High pressure over Arizona begins to gradually\nweaken and move eastward today. With less subsidence aloft, coverage\nof afternoon showers and thunderstorms looks to increase today. \nLatest hi-res guidance has the greatest coverage mainly from the \nMogollon Rim and northward, given the southwesterly flow. However,\na few storms also look possible across Yavapai County, as they \ninitiate off the Bradshaw Mountains.\n\nFlash flooding will be the primary concern with today's storms, with \n14Z RAP guidance showing PWATs around 0.80-1.10 inches. 0-6km shear \ndoes look to gradually increase (~20-25 kts) through the day across \nnorthwestern Arizona ahead of a trough from the west. However, \nMLCAPE looks to largely remain at most 500-1000 J/Kg across western \nArizona. Therefore, the threat for widespread severe weather is low, \nbit an isolated strong to severe storm can not be totally ruled out.\n\nThere is the potential for some overnight convection to move \nnorthward this evening from the Lower Deserts. However, hi-res \nguidance this morning seems to be backing off on this potential. \nNevertheless, some slight chance were maintained with the forecast \ninto the overnight hours. The best chances for this look to mainly \nbe near the I-17/US-89 corridor, potentially into Navajo and Gila \ncounties.\n\nSaturday through Monday...An active period of monsoonal conditions \nis expected this weekend into Monday. As the ridge gradually shifts \neastward, a trough over the Pacific Northwest begins to dig into the \nWest Coast. Increasing monsoonal flow will bring deep monsoonal \nmoisture northward, with PWATs exceeding an inch of the higher \nterrain to near 1.5 inches across central Arizona. Additionally with \nthe trough, added dynamics will help promote nocturnal activity into \nboth Saturday night and Sunday night.\n\nWhile storm motions of 15-25 kts will limit the overall residency \ntime of any one storm, the likelihood of numerous coverage will lead \nto a high probability of training storms. Thus, flash flood will \nremain a concern through much of the weekend. This will especially \nbe true over area fire scars, slot canyons, and other rain sensitive \nlocations.\n\nGiven the added dynamics, a few strong to severe storms will be \npossible during the afternoon hours each day. The exact locations at \ngreatest risk will largely be dependent upon clearer skies during \nthe morning and early afternoon hours, to allow for destabilization \nfrom daytime heating. The primary threat from any severe storm would \nbe large hail, with some potential for gusty winds in the lower \nelevations.\n\nOutside of the precipitation chances, cooler and breezier conditions \nare expected. Afternoon highs by Monday are forecast to be nearly 5-\n10F below normal for this time of year, with highs in the low to mid \n70s over the High Country and mid 80s to low 90s in the Lower \nDeserts.\n\nTuesday through Thursday...A fast-moving trough looks to move \nthrough Great Basin near the middle of next week. At the same time, \nTropical Storm Karina looks to initiate a surge of sub-tropical \nmoisture. The extent of the moisture push still remains in question, \ngiven how far the storm looks to be from Baja. Increased southerly \nflow with the trough however should help pull the moisture into at \nleast central Arizona. Most guidance also indicates the potential \nfor another tropical cyclone to develop near the end of the week \nbehind Karina.\n\nAs a result, continuing period of at least semi-active monsoonal \nconditions looks probable through the coming week. Uncertainty still \nremains however, with many of the GEFS, EPS, and CMCE members split \nbetween PWATs remaining high and ticking downward. The operational \nguidance does have heights tick upwards slightly, so a slight \nwarming trend seems possible, but overall seasonable to slightly \ncooler than normal temperatures look to continue.",
